<GuideLayout
  title="Which hiking stove should you choose?"
  seoTitle="Hiking Stove Guide: Canister vs Alcohol vs Solid Fuel"
  description="Hiking stove types compared: canister, alcohol, solid fuel and integrated systems, plus weight, fuel availability in Australia and total fire ban rules."
  eyebrow="Cooking and fuel"
  readTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>
    A hiking stove looks like a simple purchase until you notice how many types
    are on sale: screw-top canister stoves, tiny alcohol burners, solid fuel
    tablets and all-in-one integrated systems. Each one trades off weight, cost,
    simmer control and fuel availability differently, and the right pick for you
    depends on where you hike and how many nights you are cooking for.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is also a rule that catches out first-time overnight hikers every
    spring: what you can legally light changes on a total fire ban day, and the
    rules are not the same in every state.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Canister stoves are the simplest and most common choice for an <a
          href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight hike</a
        >: light, fast-boiling, easy to control.
      </li>
      <li>
        Alcohol and solid fuel stoves are lighter again but slower to boil and
        harder to simmer with.
      </li>
      <li>
        Integrated systems boil water fastest per gram of fuel but are less
        flexible for actual cooking.
      </li>
      <li>
        Screw-top butane-propane canisters are the easiest fuel to buy in
        Australia; metho and solid fuel tablets are also widely stocked.
      </li>
      <li>
        On a total fire ban day, wood, alcohol and solid fuel stoves are banned
        everywhere. Gas stove rules differ by state and by land manager, so
        check before you rely on one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What types of hiking stoves are there?</h2>
  <p>
    Most hiking stoves sold today fall into four groups, and understanding how
    they differ matters more than any single spec sheet.
  </p>

  <h3>Canister stoves</h3>
  <p>
    A canister stove screws onto a pre-pressurised butane-propane blend
    canister. They light with a push-button igniter or a match, boil water
    quickly and let you turn the flame down to actually simmer food rather than
    just boil water. The stove itself is usually under 100 g, though the
    canister adds weight that increases with how much fuel is left inside.
  </p>

  <h3>Alcohol stoves</h3>
  <p>
    An alcohol stove burns methylated spirits in a small metal cup, often
    homemade from a drink can or bought as a lightweight brass or titanium unit.
    There is no valve to fail and no canister to run empty gradually. The
    trade-off is a slower boil, no simmer control and a flame that is hard to
    see in bright daylight, which is a real safety consideration.
  </p>

  <h3>Solid fuel stoves</h3>
  <p>
    Solid fuel stoves burn hexamine tablets on a simple folding metal stand.
    They are the lightest and cheapest option to buy, pack flat, and never leak,
    but they are slow, produce a sooty residue on pots and give almost no
    control over heat output.
  </p>

  <h3>Integrated canister systems</h3>
  <p>
    An integrated system pairs a canister stove with a purpose-built pot that
    has a heat-exchanger base, so more of the flame's heat goes into the water
    instead of the air around it. They boil water for freeze-dried meals or hot
    drinks faster and more efficiently than a standard canister stove and pot,
    but the pot is usually a fixed narrow shape that suits boiling far better
    than actual cooking.
  </p>

