  <h2>How much does an ultralight camp stove weigh?</h2>
  <p>
    For an ultralight camp stove, the stove body itself is only part of the
    weight story. A small canister stove often weighs 70-100 g on its own, but a
    full 220 g butane-propane canister adds roughly 350 g total including the
    empty canister's own weight, and that number falls as you burn through the
    gas across a trip. Alcohol and solid fuel stoves usually weigh less than 50
    g including the stand, but you still carry fuel separately, and metho is
    denser than most hikers expect for the burn time it delivers.
  </p>
  <p>
    If pack weight matters more to you than convenience, compare total system
    weight for your trip length, not just the empty stove: stove, fuel for every
    planned meal, a pot, and a way to shield the flame from wind.
  </p>

  <h2>Is canister or alcohol better for a light weight camping stove?</h2>
  <p>
    Canister stoves suit most overnight and multi-day trips because they boil
    faster in cold or windy conditions and let you actually cook rather than
    just heat water. Alcohol stoves earn their place on short, simple trips
    where you are boiling water once or twice a day and want to shave every
    gram, and where you accept a longer wait and no real simmer.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    The lightest stove on the shelf is not always the lightest system once you
    add the fuel needed for your actual trip length.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Where can you buy hiking stove fuel in Australia?</h2>
  <p>
    Screw-top butane-propane canisters are the easiest hiking fuel to find in
    Australia. Camping and outdoor stores stock them, along with hardware stores
    and traditional retailers selling gas cartridges for camping stoves. Because
    canisters cannot be posted by air freight in many cases, buying locally near
    the start of your trip is often more reliable than ordering fuel online.
  </p>
  <p>
    Methylated spirits for alcohol stoves is sold at supermarkets and hardware
    stores nationwide, and hexamine solid fuel tablets are stocked by camping
    retailers and are lightweight enough to post. Whichever fuel you choose, buy
    it before you leave town: fuel is far harder to source once you are near a
    trailhead than in a regional centre. Plan your fuel quantity against your <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight hike packing list</a
    > rather than guessing on the day.
  </p>

  <h2>What is a total fire ban and how does it affect your stove?</h2>
  <p>
    A Total Fire Ban is a declared day, covering one or more districts, when no
    fire may be lit in the open. Every state fire authority bans wood fires, and
    typically alcohol and solid fuel stoves too, on these days without
    exception. Gas stoves are treated differently, and the exact conditions
    differ by state and even by land manager, so this is worth checking properly
    rather than assuming your last trip's rules still apply. If you are still
    assembling the rest of your kit, our <a href="/build-my-kit/"
      >Build My Kit</a
    > tool can help you plan gear around the season and region you are heading into.
  </p>

  <h3>NSW Rural Fire Service rules</h3>
  <p>
    The <a
      href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans/total-fire-ban-rules"
      rel="noopener">NSW RFS Total Fire Ban rules</a
    > describe an exemption for gas barbecues at a permanent private dwelling, kept
    under constant adult supervision with two metres of cleared ground and continuous
    water on hand. That exemption is written for home use and does not clearly extend
    to a portable stove carried on a hike. NSW National Parks' own <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/safety/camping-safety"
      rel="noopener">camping safety guidance</a
    > tells visitors to follow all fire safety rules during park fire bans and total
    fire bans, without spelling out a blanket allowance for visitor-owned gas stoves.
    Treat a Total Fire Ban day in an NSW national park as off-limits for any stove
    unless the local NPWS office or RFS Fire Control Centre confirms otherwise before
    you go.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside a Total Fire Ban, NSW also runs a statewide <a
      href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/BFDP"
      rel="noopener">Bush Fire Danger Period</a
    >, statutorily 1 October to 31 March each year (with some council areas
    starting earlier), during which a fire permit may be required for certain
    fires. Check current notices for your area before a spring or summer trip.
  </p>

  <h3>CFA Victoria rules</h3>
  <p>
    Victoria's rules are more explicit for hikers. The <a
      href="https://www.cfa.vic.gov.au/warnings-restrictions/fire-bans-ratings-and-restrictions/can-i-or-cant-i"
      rel="noopener">CFA's "Can I or Can't I" guidance</a
    > allows a commercially manufactured portable gas stove on a Total Fire Ban day,
    provided it sits on stable ground at least three metres from anything flammable,
    you have 10 litres of water or a connected hose ready, an adult stays with it
    the whole time it is alight, and it is completely extinguished before you leave.
    Solid fuel cookers, including wood and heat beads, are banned outright on a Total
    Fire Ban day anywhere in Victoria.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are hiking in a state not covered above, check your state or
    territory's fire authority before you go. Rules can also change during a
    trip, so check current declarations for the specific district you are
    walking in, not just the state as a whole.
  </p>

  <h2>What else should you carry for cooking on a hike?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A pot sized to how many people you are cooking for, not larger.</li>
    <li>
      A lighter and waterproof matches as backup, since piezo igniters do fail.
    </li>
    <li>
      Wind protection only if your stove's instructions allow it; some canister
      stoves prohibit windshields because they can overheat the fuel canister.
    </li>
    <li>Enough fuel for your trip length plus a margin for a cold snap.</li>
    <li>
      A way to check the current fire danger rating and any bans before you
      leave phone reception.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you want a complete cooking setup rather than assembling pot, stove and
    cutlery separately, our <a href="/gear/aluminium-cookware-set/"
      >aluminium cookware set review</a
    > covers a nested aluminium cook set (burner details unverified; check the listing).
    Our <a href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a> can help you fill in the rest of
    an overnight setup.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    A canister stove is the sensible default for most Australian overnight
    hikers: quick, controllable and reasonably light once you account for fuel.
    Alcohol and solid fuel stoves shave grams for simple, short trips. Whatever
    you carry, check the fire danger rating and total fire ban status for your
    route before you leave, since the rules for lighting any stove, gas
    included, are not the same in every state.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Can you use a gas stove during a total fire ban?</summary>
      <p>
        It depends on the state and the land you are on. Victoria's CFA allows a
        commercially manufactured portable gas stove under set conditions on a
        Total Fire Ban day. In NSW, the RFS exemption for gas appliances is
        written for home barbecues, and NSW national parks do not clearly extend
        a blanket allowance to portable stoves, so check with the local park
        office or RFS before relying on one.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What is the best hiking stove for beginners?</summary>
      <p>
        A simple screw-top canister stove suits most beginners. It lights
        easily, boils quickly, lets you simmer and uses fuel that is widely
        stocked at camping stores.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long does a gas canister last for hiking?</summary>
      <p>
        It depends on the canister size, flame setting and how much simmering
        versus boiling you do, so carry a spare or a slightly larger canister
        than the minimum you calculate for a multi-day trip.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Are alcohol stoves allowed on hiking trails in Australia?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Alcohol stoves are generally allowed outside fire ban periods, but they
        are banned on a Total Fire Ban day in every state, the same as wood
        fires. Always check current fire restrictions for your route.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What hiking cookware works with a canister stove?</summary>
      <p>
        Any pot with a stable base and a diameter that suits your stove's pot
        supports works, though a nested cook set keeps everything packed
        together. See our <a href="/gear/aluminium-cookware-set/"
          >aluminium cookware set review</a
        > for one nested aluminium option (burner details unverified; check the listing).
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a windshield for a hiking stove?</summary>
      <p>
        Wind can slow cooking, but check your stove's instructions before using
        a windshield. Some canister stoves prohibit any windshield because it
        can overheat the fuel canister. Choose a sheltered cooking spot if your
        stove's instructions do not allow one.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What is the NSW Bush Fire Danger Period?</summary>
      <p>
        It is the statutory period, 1 October to 31 March across most of NSW,
        when fire permits can be required for certain fires. Some council areas
        start earlier based on local conditions, so check current notices for
        your area.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
